page("texas-salt-and-pepper-rub","Texas Salt and Pepper Rub (Dalmatian Rub)","The simple Central Texas beef rub: coarse black pepper and kosher salt, ratios, and when to add garlic.","full01",f"""
<p>Central Texas brisket is famous for being seasoned with almost nothing: coarse black pepper and salt. Some pitmasters call it a Dalmatian rub for the black-and-white look. It lets beef and smoke do the talking, and it's perfect for small-grill cooks because there's nothing to burn.</p>
<h2>The ratios</h2>{table(["Style","Mix"],[["Classic","1 part kosher salt : 1 part coarse (16-mesh) black pepper, by volume"],["Peppery","1 part salt : 2 parts pepper"],["SPG","Equal parts salt, pepper and granulated garlic"]])}
<h2>How to use it</h2>{ul(["Pat meat dry. Optional: a thin smear of mustard or oil as a binder.","Shake on from about a foot above so it lands evenly.","For thick beef, season an hour ahead or the night before (uncovered in the fridge).","For steaks, fajitas and burgers, season right before cooking or 45+ minutes ahead."])}
<p>Use it on {L("brisket-flat-on-a-small-grill","brisket flat")}, {L("beef-short-ribs-on-a-folding-grill","beef short ribs")} and a {L("texas-ribeye-over-mesquite","mesquite ribeye")}.</p>
""","bbq",[("What pepper grind is used for Texas brisket?","Coarse ground black pepper; many cooks use a 16-mesh grind.")])

page("brisket-flat-on-a-small-grill","Brisket Flat on a Small Folding Grill: Smoke, Then Braise","An honest small-grill brisket method: smoke a brisket flat on the cool side of a folding charcoal grill, then finish tender in a Dutch oven.","full08",recipe(
"<p>A whole packer brisket and a 14-hour fire are not what a small, lidless folding grill is for. A <b>brisket flat</b> (3 to 5 lb) is. This method gets real smoke flavor on the grill, then finishes the beef tender in a covered cast iron Dutch oven on the same fire. It isn't a pit brisket, and we won't pretend it is, but it's a very good one, and it works at a lease or a campsite. Slot Grill says its grills are strong enough for cast iron; the <a href='"+FULL+"'>Full Size</a> gives you the most room.</p>",
"6","5–6 hours",["1 brisket flat, 3–5 lb, fat cap trimmed to about ¼&quot;","Texas salt and pepper rub (see link below)","1 cup beef broth","Lump charcoal plus 3–4 chunks of post oak","A cast iron Dutch oven large enough to hold the flat (or heavy foil)"],
["Season the brisket heavily the night before or at least an hour ahead.","Build a banked fire: lit coals at one end only. Add a wood chunk.","Set the brisket fat-side up on the cool end, as far from the coals as it'll go. Keep thin blue smoke going, adding a few lit coals and a chunk of wood every 30 to 45 minutes.","Smoke 2 to 3 hours, until the bark is dark and set and the internal temperature is around 160–170°F.","Move the brisket into the Dutch oven with the broth (or wrap tightly in foil with a splash of broth). Cover.","Set the Dutch oven over a moderate, spread-out bed of coals (not a roaring pile). Keep it gently simmering, adding lit coals as needed.","Cook until probe-tender: a thermometer probe slides in like into soft butter, usually around 200–205°F, roughly 2 to 3 more hours.","Rest, covered, at least 30 minutes (an hour is better) in a cooler with towels.","Slice across the grain about the thickness of a pencil. Spoon juices over."],
tips=["Brisket is safe long before it's tender; 145°F is the safe minimum for whole beef, but it needs 200°F-plus to break down.","Check the coals under the Dutch oven every 30 minutes; you want a simmer, not a boil.","Leftovers make great {0}.".format(L("chopped-beef-sandwiches","chopped beef sandwiches"))],size="full")+"<p>Rub: "+L("texas-salt-and-pepper-rub","Texas salt and pepper rub")+".</p>","bbq",[("Can I cook a whole packer brisket on a small grill?","It's not a good fit for a small lidless grill. A 3–5 lb flat with a smoke-then-braise finish is the practical approach.")])

page("texas-hot-links-and-smoked-sausage","Texas Hot Links and Smoked Sausage on a Folding Grill","How to cook fresh and smoked Texas sausage links on a small charcoal grill without splitting them, with safe temperatures.","c28",recipe(
"<p>Sausage is the most Texas thing you can cook on a small grill. Whether it's a coil of smoked links from a meat market or fresh jalapeño-cheddar links, the trick is gentle heat first and color last, so the casings don't burst.</p>",
"4–6","30–40 minutes",["2 lb sausage links: precooked smoked sausage, or fresh (raw) links","Pickles, sliced onion, white bread or crackers","Mustard or barbecue sauce"],
["Build a two-zone fire, with an oak or pecan chunk for smoke if you like.","Set links on the cool side. Turn every few minutes.","<b>Precooked smoked sausage:</b> heat through to 165°F, about 15 to 20 minutes.","<b>Fresh pork or beef sausage:</b> cook to 160°F; fresh chicken or turkey sausage to 165°F. Expect 20 to 30 minutes on the cool side.","Move links over the coals for the last minute or two to snap and color the casing.","Slice and serve the Texas way: pickles, onion, white bread."],
tips=["Don't poke holes; that drains the juice.","If casings split, the fire's too hot; move them farther away."]),"bbq",[("What temperature should fresh sausage reach?","Fresh pork or beef sausage 160°F; poultry sausage 165°F. Reheat precooked sausage to 165°F.")])

page("pork-spare-ribs-on-a-folding-grill","Pork Spare Ribs on a Folding Grill","Cooking pork ribs on a small lidless charcoal grill by cutting racks into sections, smoking indirect, then wrapping to finish tender.","full13",recipe(
"<p>A full rack of spare ribs won't sit away from the coals on a small grill. Cut it into 3- or 4-bone sections and it will. Smoke on the cool side, wrap to finish, and glaze at the end if you like it sticky.</p>",
"3–4","4–5 hours",["1 rack pork spare ribs (or St. Louis-cut), membrane removed","Rub: 2 tbsp each salt and coarse pepper, 1 tbsp paprika, 1 tbsp brown sugar, 1 tsp garlic powder","Splash of apple juice or broth","Barbecue sauce (optional)","Heavy-duty foil"],
["Cut the rack into 3–4 bone sections. Season all over.","Build a banked fire at one end with a hickory or pecan chunk.","Arrange rib sections bone-side down on the cool end. Keep thin smoke and a steady low fire, adding a few lit coals every 30 to 45 minutes.","Smoke about 2 to 2½ hours, rotating sections so each takes a turn closest to the heat, until the bark is set.","Wrap each section (or pairs) in foil with a splash of juice. Return to the cool side, 1 to 1½ hours.","Check tenderness: a probe should slide between bones easily, typically around 195–203°F.","Optional: unwrap, brush with sauce and set over the edge of the coals for a few minutes to tack up.","Rest 10 minutes, slice between the bones."],
tips=["Ribs are safe at 145°F but need far higher temperatures to get tender.","Sauce burns quickly over direct heat; watch it."],size="full"),"bbq",[])

page("beef-short-ribs-on-a-folding-grill","Beef Short Ribs on a Folding Grill","Chuck short ribs smoked on the cool side of a small charcoal grill and braised tender in cast iron.","full08",recipe(
"<p>Beef ribs are Texas royalty. The giant plate ribs from a pit don't suit a small grill, but meaty chuck short ribs (bone-in, 2 to 3&quot; pieces) do, using the same smoke-then-braise method as our brisket flat.</p>",
"4","4–5 hours",["3–4 lb bone-in chuck short ribs","Texas salt and pepper rub","1 cup beef broth, 1 small onion sliced","Lump charcoal and post oak chunks","Cast iron Dutch oven"],
["Season the ribs well, ideally an hour or more ahead.","Banked fire with oak. Smoke ribs on the cool side about 2 hours, turning occasionally.","Transfer to the Dutch oven with broth and onion. Cover.","Simmer over a moderate bed of coals, feeding lit coals, about 2 hours, until probe-tender (around 200–205°F).","Rest 20 minutes. Serve with the pan juices."],
tips=["Skim fat from the juices before serving.","Great on rice or in tacos."],size="full"),"bbq",[])

page("chopped-beef-sandwiches","Chopped Beef Sandwiches","Texas chopped beef sandwiches from leftover brisket or short ribs, warmed in cast iron or on a griddle plate over charcoal.","grid2",recipe(
"<p>Chopped beef is what Texas does with the brisket ends and leftovers: chop, sauce lightly, pile on a bun. It's ideal on a griddle plate or skillet over the coals the day after a brisket cook.</p>",
"4","20 minutes",["1½–2 lb leftover cooked brisket or short rib meat, chopped","½ cup barbecue sauce, plus extra","4 buns","Pickles and sliced onion"],
["Light a modest fire and preheat a <a href='"+GRID+"'>griddle plate</a> or skillet.","Add the chopped beef and a splash of water or broth; stir until hot.","Add sauce and heat through to 165°F.","Toast buns on the grate side.","Pile beef on buns; top with pickles and onion."],
tips=["Refrigerate leftovers within 2 hours of cooking (1 hour above 90°F)."]),"bbq",[])

page("resting-and-wrapping-texas-barbecue","Resting and Wrapping Texas Barbecue","Butcher paper, foil and the cooler rest: how wrapping and resting help small-grill barbecue, and how to do both safely.","full01",f"""
<p>Two steps separate tender barbecue from tough barbecue: wrapping during the cook and resting afterward.</p>
<h2>Wrapping</h2>{table(["Wrap","Effect","Best for on a small grill"],[["Foil","Traps steam; fastest, softest; softens bark","Ribs and brisket flat (the practical choice on an open grill)"],["Butcher paper","Breathes; keeps more bark","Works but cooks slower; better when you can hold steady heat"],["Dutch oven","Like foil with a lid; great on a lidless grill","Brisket flat, short ribs, chuck"]])}
<h2>Resting</h2>{ul(["Rest beef ribs and brisket at least 30 minutes, wrapped, in a dry cooler with towels.","Steaks and chops: 3 to 5 minutes.","A long rest keeps meat hot, but keep it above 140°F if holding for hours, and refrigerate leftovers within 2 hours."])}
""","bbq",[])

page("texas-bbq-plate-sides","Sides for a Texas BBQ Plate","The classic Texas barbecue plate and sides you can make at camp: pinto beans, slaw, potato salad, pickles, onions and white bread.","both02",f"""
<p>A Texas barbecue plate is meat on butcher paper with simple sides. Most can be made ahead; a couple can cook on the grill.</p>
{table(["Side","Make-ahead or on the grill"],[["Pickles, raw onion, white bread or saltines","No cooking"],["Pinto beans","Simmer in a Dutch oven on the cool side, or reheat to 165°F"],["Coleslaw","Make ahead; keep cold"],["Potato salad","Make ahead; keep at 40°F or below"],["Grilled jalapeños","On the grill, a few minutes until blistered"],["Elote (street corn)","On the grill; see {0}".format(L("grilled-street-corn","grilled street corn"))]])}
<h2>Cold sides, cold cooler</h2><p>Mayonnaise-based salads must stay cold. Keep them in a separate cooler or deep in ice, and don't leave them out more than 2 hours (1 hour above 90°F).</p>
""","bbq",[])
